course: dictionary
lesson: 12643
title: Disperse: going apart, spreading out
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: When a crowd disperses, the people walk away in different directions and the crowd is gone. Disperse is a verb, so its forms are disperse, disperses, dispersed and dispersing. «The crowd dispersed when the rain started.» The people went off in many directions, so dispersed means scattered and spread apart.
explain: Disperse can also mean spreading something widely over an area. «The farmer dispersed the seeds across the whole field.» The seeds are not in one heap any more. They are spread far and wide.
explain: Look at the clues around the word. If things are moving apart, getting thinner, or spreading out, disperse probably fits. If things are being pulled together into one group, it does not.
fact: Disperse means to go away in different directions, or to make a crowd do this.
fact: Disperse also means to spread something widely over an area.
fact: In science, seeds are dispersed by wind, water and animals.
fact: In science, light is dispersed by a prism into a rainbow of colours.
fact: «The fog dispersed by lunchtime»: for fog, cloud or smoke, disperse means to thin out and disappear.
exercise: «The police asked the crowd to disperse.» What does disperse mean here? | *To spread out and go away in different directions | To shout loudly at each other | To sing a song together | To gather together into one tight group
exercise: «By lunchtime the morning fog had dispersed.» What happened to the fog? | It turned into snow | It grew thicker and darker | It froze solid | *It thinned out and disappeared
exercise: «The wind dispersed the seeds across the meadow.» What does dispersed mean? | Hidden under the grass | Pulled into one pile | *Spread widely over the area
test: «Light passes through a prism and is dispersed into colours.» What does dispersed mean in this sentence? | *Split up and spread out into its colours | Blocked so no light can pass | Made brighter and warmer | Turned into a shadow
test: «Yesterday the children ___ quickly when the bell rang.» Which word fits the gap? | disperses | disperse | *dispersed | dispersing
exam: Which sentence uses disperse correctly? | He dispersed a loud, sweet song | The sun dispersed behind the hill | Our class dispersed into one tight group for the photo | *The guard told the crowd to disperse after the match
